__d(function(e,t,a,o,i,n,s){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"English",textbook:"A Pact with the Sun",chapter_number:6,chapter_title:"The Monkey and the Crocodile",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Where does the monkey live in the story?",options:["A) On a mango tree near a pond","B) On a rose-apple tree on the bank of a river","C) In a forest far from any water","D) On a coconut tree near the sea"],correct_answer:"B) On a rose-apple tree on the bank of a river",explanation:"The monkey lives on a rose-apple tree on the bank of a river. The sweet fruits of this tree attract the crocodile and begin the friendship. Options A, C, and D are incorrect as the story specifically mentions a rose-apple tree beside a river."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why does the crocodile's wife want the monkey's heart?",options:["A) She is jealous of the monkey's friendship with her husband","B) She believes the monkey's heart will make her live forever","C) She thinks it will be very sweet since the monkey eats rose-apples all day","D) She wants to use it as medicine for an illness"],correct_answer:"C) She thinks it will be very sweet since the monkey eats rose-apples all day",explanation:"The crocodile's wife reasons that because the monkey eats sweet rose-apples all day, his heart must be even sweeter. This greedy reasoning drives the betrayal. The other options are invented and not stated in the story."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"How does the crocodile get the monkey to sit on his back?",options:["A) He threatens the monkey with force","B) He offers the monkey a large bag of fruits","C) He falsely invites the monkey to his home across the river","D) He pretends to be injured and asks for help"],correct_answer:"C) He falsely invites the monkey to his home across the river",explanation:"The crocodile uses a false invitation \u2014 pretending to offer hospitality at his home \u2014 to lure the monkey onto his back. The monkey cannot swim, so he accepts. The other options do not appear in the story."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Where does the crocodile reveal his plan to the monkey?",options:["A) At the rose-apple tree","B) On the opposite bank of the river","C) In the middle of the river","D) At the crocodile's home underwater"],correct_answer:"C) In the middle of the river",explanation:"The crocodile reveals his plan to take the monkey's heart only when they are in the middle of the river \u2014 when the monkey is at his most vulnerable and cannot swim to safety. This makes the betrayal more dramatic."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What clever excuse does the monkey use to escape?",options:["A) He says he cannot breathe underwater","B) He tells the crocodile he left his heart on the rose-apple tree","C) He pretends to faint and falls into the water","D) He claims he is not the right monkey and the crocodile has the wrong animal"],correct_answer:"B) He tells the crocodile he left his heart on the rose-apple tree",explanation:"The monkey calmly tells the crocodile that monkeys leave their hearts on trees when they go travelling, and they would need to go back to collect it. The gullible crocodile believes this and turns back, allowing the monkey to escape."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which word best describes the crocodile's character in this story?",options:["A) Brave and loyal","B) Clever and witty","C) Gullible and disloyal","D) Kind and generous"],correct_answer:"C) Gullible and disloyal",explanation:"The crocodile is gullible because he believes the monkey's impossible claim about leaving his heart on the tree. He is disloyal because he betrays a true friend for his wife's selfish desire. The other options describe the monkey (witty) or neither character."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does 'presence of mind' mean as demonstrated by the monkey?",options:["A) Being physically strong enough to fight danger","B) Running away from danger quickly","C) Staying calm and thinking quickly when in a dangerous situation","D) Asking for help from others when in trouble"],correct_answer:"C) Staying calm and thinking quickly when in a dangerous situation",explanation:"Presence of mind is the ability to remain calm and think clearly under pressure. The monkey shows this by not panicking when the crocodile reveals his plan, and instead quickly inventing a clever escape. It is not about physical strength, running, or seeking help."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which ancient Indian collection of stories is 'The Monkey and the Crocodile' based on?",options:["A) The Ramayana","B) The Mahabharata","C) The Panchatantra","D) The Jataka Tales"],correct_answer:"C) The Panchatantra",explanation:"The story is based on a tale from the Panchatantra, compiled by Vishnu Sharma in ancient India. The Panchatantra is a collection of animal fables designed to teach wisdom. In Karnataka, Durgasimha translated it into Kannada during the Hoysala period."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What happens to the crocodile's friendship with the monkey at the end of the story?",options:["A) The monkey forgives the crocodile and they remain friends","B) The monkey ends the friendship and scolds the crocodile for his treachery","C) The crocodile apologises and the monkey returns to sit on his back","D) The monkey moves to a different tree far from the river"],correct_answer:"B) The monkey ends the friendship and scolds the crocodile for his treachery",explanation:"Once safely back on the tree, the monkey scolds the crocodile for his treachery and foolishness and permanently ends their friendship. The story shows that betrayal destroys trust and friendship irreparably."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the moral of 'The Monkey and the Crocodile'?",options:["A) Physical strength is the most important quality in life","B) Never trust anyone, even your best friends","C) Intelligence and quick thinking can overcome danger; true friendship must not be betrayed","D) Animals and humans should never become friends"],correct_answer:"C) Intelligence and quick thinking can overcome danger; true friendship must not be betrayed",explanation:"The story has two morals: the monkey's escape shows that intelligence beats physical strength, and the crocodile's loss shows that betraying a true friend for greed brings no real gain. Options A, B, and D are incorrect or too extreme."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why is the crocodile described as 'torn' before he decides to betray the monkey?",options:["A) He is physically injured by the monkey","B) He is confused about the route to his home","C) He feels conflict between loyalty to his friend and his wife's demand","D) He is unsure whether the monkey will agree to come with him"],correct_answer:"C) He feels conflict between loyalty to his friend and his wife's demand",explanation:"The crocodile is 'torn' because he knows the monkey is a true friend but his wife is pressuring him to bring the monkey's heart. He is caught between doing what is right (loyalty) and giving in to his wife's greed. He eventually chooses the wrong path."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why does the monkey's escape plan work specifically on this crocodile?",options:["A) The crocodile is afraid of the monkey and does not want to upset him","B) The crocodile's desire for the heart makes him accept the story without logical thought","C) The crocodile secretly wants to let the monkey go","D) The monkey threatens the crocodile with a weapon"],correct_answer:"B) The crocodile's desire for the heart makes him accept the story without logical thought",explanation:"The escape works because greed makes the crocodile gullible. He is so focused on the prize (the monkey's heart) that he does not stop to think about whether a monkey can really leave its heart on a tree. The monkey correctly identifies and exploits this weakness."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Who translated the Panchatantra into Kannada, and during which dynasty's rule?",options:["A) Pampa, during the Chalukya period","B) Ranna, during the Rashtrakuta period","C) Durgasimha, during the Hoysala period","D) Kumara Vyasa, during the Vijayanagara period"],correct_answer:"C) Durgasimha, during the Hoysala period",explanation:"Durgasimha translated the Panchatantra into Kannada in the 12th century during the Hoysala period. The Hoysalas were great patrons of Kannada literature and architecture. The other options name real Kannada poets but are paired with incorrect dynasties or texts."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following best describes the theme of 'wit over strength' in this story?",options:["A) The monkey wins because the crocodile decides not to fight him","B) The monkey escapes by being physically stronger and jumping far","C) The monkey escapes because he identifies and exploits the crocodile's greed and gullibility","D) The monkey is saved by other animals who come to his rescue"],correct_answer:"C) The monkey escapes because he identifies and exploits the crocodile's greed and gullibility",explanation:"The monkey cannot outswim or outfight the crocodile. His escape is entirely mental \u2014 he analyses the crocodile's character, identifies his weaknesses (greed and gullibility), and designs a plan that exploits them. This is wit defeating strength."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What does the crocodile's wife represent in the story's moral framework?",options:["A) The importance of respecting animals","B) Greed that corrupts relationships and leads to betrayal","C) The wisdom of seeking advice from family before acting","D) A reminder that wives are always right"],correct_answer:"B) Greed that corrupts relationships and leads to betrayal",explanation:"The crocodile's wife symbolises greed. Her desire for the monkey's heart \u2014 based on nothing more than a greedy assumption \u2014 sets off the entire chain of betrayal. She shows how greed can corrupt even existing, positive relationships (the monkey-crocodile friendship)."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which literary term describes a story that uses animal characters to teach a moral lesson about human behaviour?",options:["A) Biography","B) Fable","C) Autobiography","D) Novel"],correct_answer:"B) Fable",explanation:"A fable is a short story with animal characters that teaches a moral lesson about human behaviour. 'The Monkey and the Crocodile' is a fable \u2014 the monkey and crocodile represent human qualities (wit, greed, loyalty, betrayal) and the story ends with a clear moral."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"At what point in the journey does the crocodile tell the monkey about his real plan?",options:["A) Just before the monkey climbs onto his back","B) When they reach the opposite bank","C) In the middle of the river","D) When they are nearly at the crocodile's home"],correct_answer:"C) In the middle of the river",explanation:"The crocodile waits until they are in the middle of the river \u2014 where the monkey is most helpless and cannot swim to either bank \u2014 before revealing his plan. This makes the betrayal especially calculated and cruel, and the monkey's escape all the more impressive."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What quality does the monkey lack that makes him physically vulnerable in this story?",options:["A) The ability to climb trees","B) The ability to swim","C) The ability to speak","D) The ability to run fast"],correct_answer:"B) The ability to swim",explanation:"The monkey cannot swim, which is why he has to climb onto the crocodile's back to cross the river. This inability makes him physically helpless in the middle of the river \u2014 he must rely entirely on his intelligence to escape."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"The monkey's escape depends on the crocodile being both greedy and gullible. If the crocodile had been intelligent, the escape plan would have failed. What does this tell us about the monkey's approach to problem-solving?",options:["A) The monkey was lucky and did not actually plan anything","B) The monkey assessed his enemy's weaknesses before acting, making his plan targeted and effective","C) The monkey's plan was foolish and only worked by accident","D) The monkey relied on the crocodile making a mistake on his own"],correct_answer:"B) The monkey assessed his enemy's weaknesses before acting, making his plan targeted and effective",explanation:"The monkey did not use a generic escape plan \u2014 he specifically designed one that exploited the crocodile's greed (the desire for the heart) and gullibility (willingness to believe). This shows strategic thinking: identify the opponent's weakness, then design a solution around it. This is why the plan worked."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"The story shows the crocodile losing both the monkey's friendship AND his wife's wish. What universal lesson does this outcome illustrate?",options:["A) Crocodiles are naturally untrustworthy animals","B) Betrayal of trust always results in loss for the betrayer, not just the betrayed","C) One should always obey one's spouse regardless of the consequences","D) Physical strength is no guarantee of success"],correct_answer:"B) Betrayal of trust always results in loss for the betrayer, not just the betrayed",explanation:"The crocodile gains nothing from his betrayal \u2014 he neither gets the monkey's heart nor keeps his friend. This illustrates the universal moral truth that betrayal ultimately harms the betrayer most. He sacrificed a genuine, generous friendship for a greedy wish and ended up with nothing. This is the story's deepest lesson about the cost of disloyalty."}]}},2761,[]);